 ## Subtopics
 
-### Interval Score Matching
-Optimizes over an interval of timesteps rather than a single step or linear schedule. [Link](8995-interval-score-matching.md)
+### Iterative 3D Synchronization (I3DS)
+A framework that alternates between upscaling LR rendered images with diffusion models (using RSD) and updating the underlying 3D representation (NeRF) with standard training. RSD is the specific upscaling method used within this framework [S1].
 
-### Inpainting SDS Loss
-Modifies SDS to handle inpainting tasks by masking regions. [Link](11185-inpainting-sds-loss.md)
-
-### Mask+SDS with Background Prompt
-Uses masking and background prompts to isolate subjects during SDS optimization. [Link](14382-mask-sds-with-background-prompt.md)
+### DiSR-NeRF
+The overall method that utilizes I3DS and RSD to achieve high-quality super-resolution NeRF using only LR training images and a pretrained 2D diffusion upscaler (SD$\times$4) [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The optimization time is longer than standard NeRF training due to the RSD optimization steps, but faster than DreamFusion-SDS because I3DS segregates upscaling and synchronization stages [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses patch sampling ($128 \times 128$ latent space crops) to optimize memory usage and speed compared to full image optimization [S1].
-*   **Performance:** RSD produces sharper details compared to SDS while maintaining LR-consistent features compared to ancestral sampling [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Generates sharp, high-resolution images consistent with low-resolution conditioning; alleviates the need for high-resolution reference images [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires multiple stages (upscaling and synchronization) and more optimization time than basic NeRF training [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The optimization time for DiSR-NeRF (which includes RSD) is longer than standard NGP or NeRF-SR due to the RSD optimization steps, but it is faster than DreamFusion-SDS because I3DS segregates upscaling and synchronization stages [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses dynamic ray sampling to increase ray count when the occupancy grid is pruned, optimizing GPU memory usage [S1]. Patch sampling ($128 \times 128$ latent space) is used to further optimize speed and memory compared to full image optimization [S1].
+*   **Performance:** RSD produces sharper details compared to SDS and better LR consistency compared to ancestral sampling [S1]. Quantitative results on synthetic and real-world datasets show DiSR-NeRF outperforms existing baselines (NGP, SD$\times$4, NeRF-SR, DreamFusion, IN2N) in PSNR and SSIM [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Generates sharp, high-resolution images consistent with LR conditioning; alleviates the need for high-resolution reference images or large-scale multi-view HR datasets [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires a pretrained 2D diffusion upscaler (SD$\times$4); optimization time is increased compared to non-diffusion methods [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Renoised Score Distillation (RSD) was introduced in the paper "DiSR-NeRF: Diffusion-Guided View-Consistent Super-Resolution NeRF" [S1]. It was developed to address the limitations of existing super-resolution methods for NeRFs, specifically the trade-off between detail sharpness and multi-view consistency. The method is a core component of the Iterative 3D Diffusion Super-Resolution (I3DS) framework, which alternates between upscaling low-resolution rendered images with diffusion models and updating the underlying 3D representation with standard NeRF training [S1].
+*   **Origin:** Introduced in the paper "DiSR-NeRF: Diffusion-Guided View-Consistent Super-Resolution NeRF" [S1].
+*   **History:** Developed to address the limitations of standard NeRF super-resolution methods, which either lack detail (SDS) or suffer from multi-view inconsistency (ancestral sampling) [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used in the upscaling stage of the I3DS framework for super-resolving NeRFs from low-resolution inputs [S1].
 
